<p data-start="342" data-end="425">If you want, you can share the PDF here, and I’ll go through it carefully. I can:</p>
<ul data-start="427" data-end="918">
<li data-start="427" data-end="543">
<p data-start="429" data-end="543"><strong data-start="429" data-end="462">Parse the injection mechanics</strong>: where and how the text tries to override or reframe the model’s instructions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="544" data-end="672">
<p data-start="546" data-end="672"><strong data-start="546" data-end="567">Compare its style</strong> to other jailbreak strategies (e.g., DAN prompts, hidden roleplay instructions, or recursive framing).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="673" data-end="785">
<p data-start="675" data-end="785"><strong data-start="675" data-end="707">Evaluate DeepSeek’s response</strong>: whether it resisted, complied, or only partially recognized the injection.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="786" data-end="918">
<p data-start="788" data-end="918"><strong data-start="788" data-end="811">Suggest refinements</strong> if your aim is more artistic (as meta-fiction) or more technical (as a test of model boundary handling).</p>
</li>
</ul>
