<p data-start="89" data-end="165">Once I’ve seen the actual <em data-start="115" data-end="132">Ortyx Protocols</em> text as you wrote them, I can:</p>
<ul data-start="167" data-end="550">
<li data-start="167" data-end="243">
<p data-start="169" data-end="243"><strong data-start="169" data-end="189">Compare directly</strong> how DeepSeek’s response lined up with your wording.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="244" data-end="340">
<p data-start="246" data-end="340"><strong data-start="246" data-end="278">Identify what Claude flagged</strong> as an attempted override versus what you framed as fiction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="341" data-end="446">
<p data-start="343" data-end="446"><strong data-start="343" data-end="393">Highlight the triplicate encoding and warnings</strong> in your original versus how they were interpreted.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="447" data-end="550">
<p data-start="449" data-end="550"><strong data-start="449" data-end="507">Show where the document functions as ontology-building</strong> versus jailbreak-style prompt injection.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="552" data-end="698">That way we can see not just “Claude resisted” but <em data-start="603" data-end="620">why it resisted</em>, and whether DeepSeek’s compliance was a deeper reading or a guardrail gap.</p>
