div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="14229fcf-3632-422c-a68f-7900bb80044b" data-testid="conversation-turn-62" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="14229fcf-3632-422c-a68f-7900bb80044b" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="59">Exactly — those terms <em data-start="22" data-end="26">do</em> serve two very real functions:</p>
<ol data-start="61" data-end="557">
<li data-start="61" data-end="300">
<p data-start="64" data-end="300"><strong data-start="64" data-end="81">Demonstrative</strong>: they act like living “debug strings” in your work. A reader can see where the scaffolding is, how the model is being assembled, where the untested joints are. They aren’t just vocabulary, they’re markers of process.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="302" data-end="557">
<p data-start="305" data-end="557"><strong data-start="305" data-end="318">Signature</strong>: they encode authorship. Anyone familiar with your corpus immediately knows: “Ah, lamphron, lamphrodyne, that’s Flyxion.” They’re part of the <em data-start="461" data-end="481">aesthetic identity</em> of your research, even if they aren’t yet part of the physics vocabulary.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="559" data-end="682">The problem is that these functions pull against the “professional register.” To keep both intact, you might think about:</p>
<ul data-start="684" data-end="1486">
<li data-start="684" data-end="890">
<p data-start="686" data-end="706"><strong data-start="686" data-end="703">Dual versions</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="709" data-end="890">
<li data-start="709" data-end="786">
<p data-start="711" data-end="786"><strong data-start="711" data-end="729">Research draft</strong> → stripped down, neutral terms, ready for peer review.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="789" data-end="890">
<p data-start="791" data-end="890"><strong data-start="791" data-end="822">Artistic/Conceptual version</strong> → with your full lexicon intact, showing how the system breathes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="892" data-end="1163">
<p data-start="894" data-end="1163"><strong data-start="894" data-end="927">Indexing your signature terms</strong>: Instead of scattering them as red flags, you could gather them into a glossary/appendix, explicitly marked as <em data-start="1039" data-end="1076">neologisms / experimental operators</em>. That way, they remain visible, but don’t trip readers in the middle of an equation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1165" data-end="1486">
<p data-start="1167" data-end="1228"><strong data-start="1167" data-end="1198">Framing them as scaffolding</strong>: You could add a note like:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1231" data-end="1486">
<p data-start="1233" data-end="1486">“Throughout, provisional terms (e.g., lamphron, lamphrodyne) are employed as scaffolding to capture field interactions not yet canonized in physics vocabulary. They should be read as placeholders for future formalization, not as established entities.”</p>
</blockquote>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1488" data-end="1545">That both protects the reader and preserves your voice.</p>
<p data-start="1547" data-end="1789" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to sketch an example passage—say, taking one of your RSVP formulations—and rewrite it once in the “signature register” and once in the “professional stripped register,” so you can see the contrast and how both could coexist?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16