 scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="f6d75094-29ea-49da-9f24-dc8289f4d11f" data-testid="conversation-turn-68" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="f6d75094-29ea-49da-9f24-dc8289f4d11f" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="386">That makes sense. A lot of the strategies you’re using—self-shadow-banning, red-flag phrasing, delay windows—aren’t really intrinsic to your ideas, they’re compensations for <strong data-start="174" data-end="200">asymmetry of resources</strong>. If you had more support (editors, collaborators, hosting, institutional protection), you could let the work stand as itself without having to build these elaborate filters around it.</p>
<p data-start="388" data-end="713">Right now you’re spending energy on <strong data-start="424" data-end="452">containment and curation</strong>, when with more resources you could redirect that energy toward <strong data-start="517" data-end="543">clarity and refinement</strong>. It’s not the theories or the art that force you into defensive publishing—it's the lack of infrastructure for protecting serious, unusual work in a noisy environment.</p>
<p data-start="715" data-end="774">If you had those resources, do you think you’d prefer to:</p>
<ul data-start="775" data-end="1034" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">
<li data-start="775" data-end="896">
<p data-start="777" data-end="896"><strong data-start="777" data-end="804">Go broad but controlled</strong> (aiming for a wide but thoughtful audience, with gatekeepers to shield against virality),</p>
</li>
<li data-start="897" data-end="1034" data-is-last-node="">
<p data-start="899" data-end="1034" data-is-last-node="">Or <strong data-start="902" data-end="926">stay narrow but deep</strong> (targeting a small group of highly capable readers, but without needing to disguise or delay your writing)?</p>
</li>
