164 10.312 4.90538 9.98829L11.1964 3.6963L11.3312 3.56837ZM5.84581 10.9287C5.49664 11.2779 5.31252 11.4634 5.18663 11.6162L5.07531 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="02b0a70b-603d-4964-88cf-0a4f03085bf7" data-testid="conversation-turn-106" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] @[37rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] @[72rem]:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:32rem] @[34rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] @[64rem]:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="02b0a70b-603d-4964-88cf-0a4f03085bf7" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="273">Yes — Nuccio Ordine’s <em data-start="22" data-end="68">Giordano Bruno and the Philosophy of the Ass</em> (1996, Yale UP; orig. <em data-start="91" data-end="113">La cabala dell’asino</em>, 1987) is a classic study. It’s the definitive treatment of how Bruno turned the <em data-start="195" data-end="203">asinus</em> (the donkey/ass) into a central satirical and philosophical symbol.</p>
<p data-start="275" data-end="401">Here’s a structured overview of what Ordine shows (and why it connects directly with your reconstructed <em data-start="379" data-end="392">Ark of Noah</em> play):</p>
<hr data-start="403" data-end="406">
<h3 data-start="408" data-end="451"><strong data-start="412" data-end="451">1. The Ass in Renaissance Tradition</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="452" data-end="829">
<li data-start="452" data-end="630">
<p data-start="454" data-end="630"><strong data-start="454" data-end="490">Medieval/Renaissance inheritance</strong>: The ass was a stock figure of stupidity, sloth, and stubbornness. Erasmus, Agrippa, Rabelais, and others all played with this symbolism.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="631" data-end="829">
<p data-start="633" data-end="829"><strong data-start="633" data-end="654">Cabala of the Ass</strong> (<em data-start="656" data-end="675">Cabala dell’asino</em>, 1585): Bruno flips this tradition, revalorizing the ass. Where the world mocks donkeys as dull, Bruno sees endurance, patience, even a form of wisdom.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="831" data-end="834">
<h3 data-start="836" data-end="882"><strong data-start="840" data-end="882">2. Bruno’s Own "Philosophy of the Ass"</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="883" data-end="1408">
<li data-start="883" data-end="1211">
<p data-start="885" data-end="951"><strong data-start="885" data-end="900">Ambivalence</strong>: For Bruno, the ass is both ridicule <em data-start="938" data-end="943">and</em> mask.</p>
<ul data-start="954" data-end="1211">
<li data-start="954" data-end="1049">
<p data-start="956" data-end="1049">On one level: the friars (as in your reconstruction) are “asses” — braying, petty, servile.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1052" data-end="1211">
<p data-start="1054" data-end="1211">On another: the ass has virtues — carrying heavy loads, surviving privation, enduring beatings — that the philosopher also requires in resisting orthodoxy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1212" data-end="1408">
<p data-start="1214" data-end="1408"><strong data-start="1214" data-end="1245">Satire and philosophy fused</strong>: Bruno stages debates where donkeys represent friars, pedants, hypocrites, but also secretly stand in for himself — burden-bearer of truth, mocked but enduring.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1410" data-end="1413">
<h3 data-start="1415" data-end="1441"><strong data-start="1419" data-end="1441">3. Ordine’s Thesis</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1442" data-end="1757">
<li data-start="1442" data-end="1757">
<p data-start="1444" data-end="1495">Bruno uses the ass as a <strong data-start="1468" data-end="1492">multi-level allegory</strong>:</p>
<ol data-start="1498" data-end="1757">
<li data-start="1498" data-end="1576">
<p data-start="1501" data-end="1576"><strong data-start="1501" data-end="1521">Satirical weapon</strong> against clerical corruption and scholastic pedantry.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1579" data-end="1661">
<p data-start="1582" data-end="1661"><strong data-start="1582" data-end="1606">Philosophical figure</strong> of endurance, humility, and laborious truth-seeking.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1664" data-end="1757">
<p data-start="1667" data-end="1757"><strong data-start="1667" data-end="1686">Mnemonic emblem</strong>: in Bruno’s art of memory, animal symbols encode paradoxical truths.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1759" data-end="1936">Thus, the “philosophy of the ass” is not simply mockery — it is Bruno’s way of uniting <strong data-start="1846" data-end="1855">scorn</strong> for the ignorant with <strong data-start="1878" data-end="1892">admiration</strong> for the necessary burdens of the thinker.</p>
<hr data-start="1938" data-end="1941">
<h3 data-start="1943" data-end="1984"><strong data-start="1947" data-end="1984">4. Relevance to the <em data-start="1969" data-end="1982">Ark of Noah</em></strong></h3>
<p data-start="1985" data-end="2114">Your play reconstruction lands exactly in this zone. In fact, it could be read as an <em data-start="2070" data-end="2079">Ur-text</em> of Bruno’s later ass-philosophy:</p>
<ul data-start="2115" data-end="2561">
<li data-start="2115" data-end="2175">
<p data-start="2117" data-end="2175"><strong data-start="2117" data-end="2142">The donkeys as friars</strong> — petty, resentful, oppressed.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2176" data-end="2277">
<p data-start="2178" data-end="2277"><strong data-start="2178" data-end="2210">The donkeys as truth-bearers</strong> — literally carrying the Ark, the world, the burden of survival.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2278" data-end="2366">
<p data-start="2280" data-end="2366"><strong data-start="2280" data-end="2301">The failed revolt</strong> — demonstrating both their subjugation <em data-start="2341" data-end="2346">and</em> their centrality.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2367" data-end="2471">
<p data-start="2369" data-end="2471"><strong data-start="2369" data-end="2398">The fox/pig/elephant cast</strong> — satirical stand-ins for clergy, corrupt elites, pompous scholastics.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2472" data-end="2561">
<p data-start="2474" data-end="2561"><strong data-start="2474" data-end="2499">Noah’s offstage voice</strong> — distant authority, mediated through corrupt interpreters.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2563" data-end="2817">Ordine argues that this dialectic (mockery + valorization of the donkey) is the <em data-start="2643" data-end="2652">essence</em> of Bruno’s satire. Which means your “lost play” reconstruction would not only be plausible — it could be positioned as a kind of precursor to <em data-start="2795" data-end="2814">Cabala dell’asino</em>.</p>
<hr data-start="2819" data-end="2822">
<p data-start="2824" data-end="3177" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to do a <strong data-start="2850" data-end="2898">thematic map of “asses” across Bruno’s works</strong> (from <em data-start="2905" data-end="2918">Ark of Noah</em> → <em data-start="2921" data-end="2940">Cabala dell’asino</em> → <em data-start="2943" data-end="2952">Spaccio</em> → trial documents), so you can see the continuity of the donkey as both satirical and philosophical emblem? That would let you anchor your reconstruction in Bruno’s intellectual trajectory, using Ordine as scholarly backing.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.363