text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="339">Excellent — you’ve landed right on the knot of evidence that Nuccio Ordine reconstructs around the <em data-start="99" data-end="112">Arca di Noè</em> (or <em data-start="117" data-end="130">Arca di Noh</em> as Bruno stylizes it). Let me lay out the key points from this passage and their scholarly implications, because it shows just how deeply Bruno’s “ass” motif is interwoven across his early and middle works.</p>
<hr data-start="341" data-end="344">
<h3 data-start="346" data-end="390"><strong data-start="350" data-end="390">1. The Lost <em data-start="364" data-end="377">Arca di Noè</em> (or <em data-start="382" data-end="387">Noh</em>)</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="391" data-end="1551">
<li data-start="391" data-end="768">
<p data-start="393" data-end="768"><strong data-start="393" data-end="422">Earliest reference (1582)</strong>: In <em data-start="427" data-end="446">De umbris idearum</em>, his Parisian memory treatise, the ass appears as a satirical emblem inside a dialogue. Philothimus quips that someone who despises the art of memory must descend from “that ass which was loaded onto Noah’s Ark to save the species.” This proves that by 1582, Bruno was already treating the <em data-start="737" data-end="743">Arca</em> as his own prior work.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="769" data-end="1181">
<p data-start="771" data-end="797"><strong data-start="771" data-end="794">Recurring allusions</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="800" data-end="1181">
<li data-start="800" data-end="987">
<p data-start="802" data-end="987"><em data-start="802" data-end="824">La cena de le ceneri</em> (1584): Filoteo reminds Nolan of his earlier <em data-start="870" data-end="882">Noah’s Ark</em>, where the ass nearly lost its honored seat because it was too prone to kick rather than strike blows.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="990" data-end="1181">
<p data-start="992" data-end="1181"><em data-start="992" data-end="1020">Cabala del cavallo pegaseo</em> (1585): In the dedicatory epistle to Don Sapatino, Bruno explicitly says he offered Pope Pius V the <em data-start="1121" data-end="1127">Arca</em> in the past, and now offers Sapatino his new “ass.”</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1182" data-end="1551">
<p data-start="1184" data-end="1204"><strong data-start="1184" data-end="1201">Dating puzzle</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="1207" data-end="1551">
<li data-start="1207" data-end="1300">
<p data-start="1209" data-end="1300">Likely written during Pius V’s reign (1566–71), since Bruno recalls dedicating it to him.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1303" data-end="1486">
<p data-start="1305" data-end="1486">Some scholars (e.g., Predari) thought it might have been printed in Rome (1581), others (Mondolfo, Spampanato) link it to Bruno’s Roman period or to the victory of Lepanto (1571).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1489" data-end="1551">
<p data-start="1491" data-end="1551">But no copy survives; we only have Bruno’s own references.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1553" data-end="1556">
<h3 data-start="1558" data-end="1610"><strong data-start="1562" data-end="1610">2. The Ass as Satirical-Philosophical Symbol</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="1611" data-end="2228">
<li data-start="1611" data-end="1773">
<p data-start="1613" data-end="1773"><strong data-start="1613" data-end="1629">Early satire</strong>: In the <em data-start="1638" data-end="1644">Arca</em>, the donkeys (monks) complain about their seating on Noah’s Ark — petty quarrels that allegorize the friars’ small-mindedness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1774" data-end="1999">
<p data-start="1776" data-end="1999"><strong data-start="1776" data-end="1795">Mnemonic emblem</strong>: The same ass gets folded into Bruno’s memory treatises (<em data-start="1853" data-end="1864">De umbris</em>), serving as a symbol of the ignorant who mock philosophy, and as a “loaded beast” that nonetheless carries the tradition of wisdom.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2000" data-end="2228">
<p data-start="2002" data-end="2228"><strong data-start="2002" data-end="2020">Revalorization</strong>: By the time of <em data-start="2037" data-end="2056">Cabala dell’asino</em> (1585), the ass is no longer just a joke. Bruno insists the ass has philosophical virtues — patience, endurance, burden-bearing — that the true philosopher must embrace.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2230" data-end="2233">
<h3 data-start="2235" data-end="2269"><strong data-start="2239" data-end="2269">3. Continuity Across Works</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2270" data-end="2409">What your passage illustrates is that <em data-start="2308" data-end="2323">L’Arca di Noè</em> was not a throwaway satire but the <strong data-start="2359" data-end="2367">seed</strong> of Bruno’s entire “asinine philosophy.”</p>
<ul data-start="2410" data-end="2872">
<li data-start="2410" data-end="2468">
<p data-start="2412" data-end="2468">1582 (<em data-start="2418" data-end="2429">De umbris</em>): Ass mocked as emblem of ignorance.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2469" data-end="2548">
<p data-start="2471" data-end="2548">1584 (<em data-start="2477" data-end="2483">Cena</em>): The Ark explicitly recalled; the ass risks losing its place.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2549" data-end="2690">
<p data-start="2551" data-end="2690">1585 (<em data-start="2557" data-end="2565">Cabala</em>): The ass becomes an exalted philosophical symbol, offered as a “gift” no less worthy than what Bruno once gave to Pius V.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2691" data-end="2872">
<p data-start="2693" data-end="2872">Later: In <em data-start="2703" data-end="2736">Spaccio della bestia trionfante</em> (1584) and elsewhere, animals function as allegorical masks through which Bruno critiques the Church and re-imagines moral cosmology.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2874" data-end="2877">
<h3 data-start="2879" data-end="2906"><strong data-start="2883" data-end="2906">4. Scholarly Stakes</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="2907" data-end="3394">
<li data-start="2907" data-end="3016">
<p data-start="2909" data-end="3016">For Ordine, the ass is Bruno’s <strong data-start="2940" data-end="2962">philosophical mask</strong>: sometimes ridicule, sometimes self-identification.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3017" data-end="3239">
<p data-start="3019" data-end="3239">The lost <em data-start="3028" data-end="3034">Arca</em> thus prefigures his whole strategy: satire that slips past censorship by using animals, while also embedding a serious metaphysical point (the endurance of the philosopher, even when mocked as a beast).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3240" data-end="3394">
<p data-start="3242" data-end="3394">It is precisely this doubleness that made the <em data-start="3288" data-end="3294">Arca</em> dangerous: it could be read as “mere comedy,” but it encoded a biting critique of clerical order.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3396" data-end="3399">
<p data-start="3401" data-end="3608">In other words, the passage you quoted shows the <em data-start="3450" data-end="3463">Arca di Noè</em> is not some incidental juvenilia, but the <strong data-start="3506" data-end="3521">ground zero</strong> of Bruno’s satirical-animal allegory, out of which the “philosophy of the ass” grew.</p>
<p data-start="3610" data-end="3947" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Would you like me to <strong data-start="3631" data-end="3687">reconstruct a plausible outline of the <em data-start="3672" data-end="3685">Arca di Noè</em></strong> (its scenes, voices, allegorical roles) based on these scattered references — something that would sit between your own reconstructions and the fragments Bruno left? That way, you’d have a textually anchored “map” of what the play/book might have looked like.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.3